<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1114–<a href="/years/1162">1162</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-barcelona">House of Barcelona</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramon Berenguer IV was Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> from 1131. His betrothal in 1137 to Petronilla, heiress of Aragon, joined Aragon and Catalonia in the dynastic union later called the Crown of Aragon. He took part in the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> in 1147 and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> in 1148 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a> in 1149, pushing the frontier down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-barcelona.jpg" alt="Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona" width="320" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RB4_1140.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ramon Berenguer IV, sometimes called the Saint, was born in 1114, the son of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iii-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer III of Barcelona</a> and Countess Douce I of Provence. He inherited the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> from his father on 19 August 1131. On 11 August 1137, aged about 24, he was betrothed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon">Petronilla of Aragon</a>, who was then one year old. Her father, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-ii-of-aragon">Ramiro II of Aragon</a>, had sought Barcelona's aid against Alfonso VII of León, and on 13 November 1137 he withdrew from public life, leaving his kingdom to Petronilla and Ramon Berenguer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ramon Berenguer never took the title of king, though he used it at least once, in 1139. He commonly styled himself Count of the Barcelonans and Prince of the Aragonians, and occasionally Marquis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a>. Historians differ on his position: one holds that he ruled Aragon for Petronilla as prince consort, another that he ruled as sovereign prince, and a third that his authority was subordinate to hers and to Ramiro's. His treaty with Ramiro stipulated that their descendants would rule both realms jointly, each keeping its own laws and institutions. Historians consider this arrangement the political masterstroke of the Hispanic Middle Ages, because it gave Aragon an outlet to the sea.</p><p>In October 1147, as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>, he helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>. In December 1148 he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> after a six-month siege, with the help of southern French, Anglo-Norman and Genoese crusaders, and the next year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraga">Fraga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a> and Mequinenza fell to him after a seven-month siege. He also acted as regent of Provence between 1144 and 1157 for his nephew Ramon Berenguer II. In 1151 he signed the Treaty of Tudilén with Alfonso VII, which defined the two rulers' zones of conquest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, and he founded the royal monastery of Poblet. In 1154 he accepted the regency of Gaston V of Béarn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ramon Berenguer IV died on 6 August 1162 at Borgo San Dalmazzo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. Petronilla succeeded him, and when she abdicated in 1164 their eldest surviving son, Ramon Berenguer, inherited Aragon and took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-aragon">Alfonso II of Aragon</a>. His younger son Pere inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> and the lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, and took the name Ramon Berenguer. The Chronicle of San Juan de la Peña described him as a man of great nobility, prudence and probity, and large and well proportioned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His daughter Petronila was betrothed in 1137, when still an infant, to <b>Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona</b>, who governed Aragon as its prince while keeping his own counties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Alfonso I's death, his brother Ramiro II, a monk recalled from the cloister to rule, fathered a daughter, Petronila, and in 1137 betrothed her to <b>Ramon Berenguer IV</b>, count of Barcelona.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Ramon Berenguer I extended Barcelona’s authority in the eleventh century, and <b>Ramon Berenguer IV</b> conquered Tortosa in 1148 and Lleida in 1149, completing what became known as New Catalonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The young commune also fought for the western Mediterranean, joining Alfonso VII of Castile against Almería and <b>Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona</b> against Tortosa in 1146–1148.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1147 Alfonso VII of Castile, styled Emperor of Spain, led a coalition including Genoa, Pisa, <b>Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona</b> and García Ramírez of Navarre against Almería, then a corsair base.</blockquote><a href="/state/castilian-almeria">Castilian Almería</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1137 <b>Ramon Berenguer IV</b> was betrothed to Petronilla, heiress of Aragon, and governed both lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-barcelona">County of Barcelona</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Borgo San Dalmazzo, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.38,2.18&amp;year=1145" aria-label="Barcelona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4603 688 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5053 958h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5053 958h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.38,2.18&amp;year=1145">What was here in 1145? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
